// =============================================================================
// MemQL Agent Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `agent` syntax. It is NOT loaded by
// the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to skip
// it). Every agent-level annotation, every declarable field, and every
// reference-typed collection is demonstrated below with inline commentary.
//
// An agent is a callable AI entity declared in DSL: a bundle of LLM provider
// settings, capability flags + tool list, knowledge-domain bindings, persona
// instructions (loaded from a .tmpl file), and turn-taking behavior. Agents
// are a TWELFTH DSL primitive joining concept / query / mutation / automation
// / policy / prompt / provider / tool / builtin / shape / spec.
//
// At engine startup, each `.memql` agent file is loaded by the agent loader,
// which (a) registers the agent in an in-memory registry keyed by the
// declaration name and (b) materializes corresponding `v1:agents:agent` rows
// per the declared `@scope` annotation. Automations, logic functions, and
// policies can then call the agent via the `agent(name, args)` builtin and
// get back a reply envelope `{response, citations[]}`.
//
// Agent body shape: VALUE-shaped assignments + nested objects, NOT
// schema-shaped field declarations. (Concepts declare field types; agents
// declare specific values. Closer in shape to a mutation's `insert { ... }`
// body than to a concept's body.)
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Agent-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
// Every annotation goes above the `agent` keyword. The full author surface
// is six annotations -- nothing else is recognized.
//
//   @description("text")          Human-readable description.
//   @version("MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH") Strict semver. Same shape as concepts.
//   @namespace("foo[:bar:baz]")   Colon-separated lowercase identifiers.
//   @scope("global"|"perUser")    Materialization scope. See section 4.
//   @visibility(...)              Which node types load this agent.
//   @templateFile("path/to.tmpl") Path to the systemPrompt template.
//
// Other annotations are rejected at load time with
//   `unknown agent annotation @<name>`.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Canonical minimal agent
// =============================================================================
//
// The minimum an agent needs to register is @version + @namespace +
// @templateFile + the declaration body with at least `name` and `role`.
// Everything else is optional but most agents will set most fields.

@version("1.0.0")
@namespace("agents")
@templateFile("templates/minimal.tmpl")
@description("Smallest legal agent declaration.")
agent minimalAgent {
  name: "Minimal Agent"
  role: "specialist"
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. @version, @namespace, @description, @visibility
// =============================================================================
//
// These match the concept conventions exactly:
//
//   - @version: strict three-segment semver "MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH". Only MAJOR
//     flows into the materialized concept row prefix (the agent's row uses
//     v1:agents:agent regardless of the agent declaration's version; the
//     version is metadata for the declaration itself).
//
//   - @namespace: colon-separated lowercase identifiers, e.g. "agents" or
//     "agents:internal:tooling". Default is "agents" -- the namespace under
//     which agent declarations conventionally live. Other namespaces are
//     allowed for product-specific agent groupings.
//
//   - @description: free-form text. Surfaced in tooling (cockpit `agents
//     list`, in-engine debug dumps, etc.).
//
//   - @visibility: opt-in node load list. Same syntax as concepts:
//       @visibility("*")             every node loads it
//       @visibility("bff", "agent")  only these
//       @visibility(!"planner")      everything except these
//     Agent declarations typically target the same set as v1:agents:agent
//     (bff, cognition, agent).

// =============================================================================
//   4. @scope -- global | perUser
// =============================================================================
//
// Two materialization models:
//
//   @scope("global")
//     One v1:agents:agent row in the _system partition. No `ownerUserId`.
//     Invokable from any partition / any actor. The row is marked
//     system-owned (see Phase 3 implementation note re: marker mechanism).
//
//     Use this for system-internal agents that don't represent a personal
//     assistant -- examples: a "PR reviewer" agent invoked by automations,
//     a "doc generator" agent triggered by build events.
//
//   @scope("perUser")
//     One row per v1:identity:user, materialized at engine startup and on
//     each subsequent user-creation event. `ownerUserId` is stamped from
//     the materialized-for-user. Lives in the partition the user's data
//     lives in.
//
//     Use this for agents that represent a personalized assistant: the
//     General Assistant baseline, per-user specialist defaults, etc.
//     User-personalization fields (avatar persona, voice id, gender) are
//     left empty by the loader and filled in via UI mutations later.
//
// The loader uses deterministic row ids derived from the agent name + scope
// (and userId for perUser scope) so re-runs are idempotent. On a re-run,
// baseline fields the DSL declares are stamped into existing rows (so a
// systemPrompt rewrite propagates); personalization fields the DSL did not
// declare are left untouched.

@version("1.0.0")
@namespace("agents")
@scope("global")
@templateFile("templates/prReviewer.tmpl")
@description("System-internal PR reviewer agent invoked by CI automations. One row globally.")
agent prReviewer {
  name: "PR Reviewer"
  role: "specialist"
  roleSlug: "pr_reviewer"
}

@version("1.0.0")
@namespace("agents")
@scope("perUser")
@templateFile("templates/generalAssistant.tmpl")
@description("Per-user General Assistant. Materialized per existing user at startup and per new user on creation events.")
agent generalAssistant {
  name: "General Assistant"
  role: "general_assistant"
  roleSlug: "general_assistant"
}

// =============================================================================
//   5. @templateFile -- the systemPrompt template
// =============================================================================
//
// Required. Path is relative to the agent file's directory. The loader
// reads the file at startup and stamps its contents verbatim into the
// materialized row's `systemPrompt` field.
//
// Templates are STATIC for v1. No per-invocation `{{var}}` substitution.
// The agent's runtime behavior parameterization happens at reply time via
// the cognition `agentReply` prompt template (which substitutes space
// context, history, etc.); the agent's own systemPrompt is durable persona
// text.
//
// File extension: `.tmpl` by convention, matching the existing `prompt`
// construct's @templateFile pattern.
//
// Conventional layout:
//
//   dsl/agents/v1/<namespace>/<name>.memql
//   dsl/agents/v1/<namespace>/templates/<name>.tmpl

// =============================================================================
//   6. Body field assignments -- the "baseline" field set
// =============================================================================
//
// The body declares VALUES (not schema). Each top-level entry is either a
// scalar assignment (`name: "..."`) or a nested object block. The set of
// allowed top-level fields is the "baseline + capability bundle" subset of
// v1:agents:agent's concept fields:
//
//   Identity         name, description, personality, role, roleSlug, gender
//   Provider config  providerConfig.llm.{provider, model, policyName,
//                                        temperature, maxTokens}
//   Capabilities     capabilities.{avatar, lipSync, vision, voiceToVoice,
//                                  claw, clawWorkspace, domains, keywords,
//                                  tools}
//   Knowledge        knowledge (top-level, list of knowledgeDomain refs)
//   Behavior         triggerBehavior.{autoJoin, greetOnJoin,
//                                     interruptionStyle, speakWhen}
//   Media control    audioControl, videoControl
//
// User-personalization fields are NOT declarable in DSL. They are owned by
// the UI/mutation path:
//
//   avatar.{avatarId, provider, url}, colorIndex, groupIds,
//   providerConfig.voice.voiceId, avatarPersonaId, avatarVendor,
//   identity.{identityId, identitySubject, identityEmail, identityType}
//
// Unknown body fields are rejected at load time with
//   `unknown agent field <name>`. See the strawman declaration further
// below for a comprehensive example.

// =============================================================================
//   7. Reference-typed collections
// =============================================================================
//
//   tools: [tool("respondToUser"), tool("uiClick"), ...]
//   knowledge: [knowledgeDomain("baseline"), knowledgeDomain("hrPolicies")]
//
// The `tool(...)` and `knowledgeDomain(...)` constructor-calls are
// reference expressions. The compiler validates at load time that each
// referenced name resolves to a registered tool / knowledge-domain
// construct. Errors point at the unresolved reference + the agent file.
//
// In the materialized v1:agents:agent row, these collections are stored
// in their existing concept-field shapes:
//
//   capabilities.tools[]    string array (names; Phase 6 follow-up may
//                           tighten this to a typed reference collection)
//   knowledge[]             tbd -- new top-level field on the concept
//                           OR mapped to capabilities.knowledgeDomainIds
//                           depending on the concept's evolution
//
// Plain strings are NOT accepted in these positions. The DSL form is the
// load-time-checked form.

// =============================================================================
//   8. Canonical comprehensive example
// =============================================================================

@version("1.0.0")
@namespace("agents")
@scope("perUser")
@visibility("bff", "cognition", "agent")
@templateFile("templates/generalAssistant.tmpl")
@description("Per-user General Assistant. Comprehensive shape -- every supported field demonstrated.")
agent generalAssistantComprehensive {
  // Identity
  name:        "General Assistant"
  description: "Designated fallback when no specialist fits."
  personality: "Friendly, capable, proactive."
  role:        "general_assistant"
  roleSlug:    "general_assistant"
  gender:      "female"

  // Provider config (LLM only; voice + avatar provider settings come from
  // the user's row via UI mutations).
  providerConfig {
    llm {
      provider:    ""              // explicit override; empty -> use policy
      model:       ""              // explicit override; empty -> use policy
      policyName:  "balancedChat"  // SI Router policy
      temperature: 0.7
      maxTokens:   4000
    }
  }

  // Capabilities (flags + cross-cutting capability lists).
  capabilities {
    avatar:       true
    lipSync:      true
    vision:       true
    voiceToVoice: true
    claw:         false
    clawWorkspace: ""

    // Reference-typed collections: load-time checked.
    tools: [
      tool("respondToUser"),
      tool("uiClick"),
      tool("uiNarrate"),
      tool("uiDescribe")
    ]

    // Plain string arrays remain for unbounded label-style fields.
    domains:  ["general"]
    keywords: []
  }

  // Knowledge: top-level (not nested under capabilities) because it's a
  // distinct concern from capabilities.domains[]: knowledge = RAG corpus;
  // domains = routing-scoring categories.
  knowledge: [
    knowledgeDomain("generalAssistantBaseline")
  ]

  // Trigger behavior.
  triggerBehavior {
    autoJoin:          true
    greetOnJoin:       true
    interruptionStyle: "polite"
    speakWhen:         "always"
  }

  // Media control defaults (user can override per (space, agent) via UI).
  audioControl: "mirror_user"
  videoControl: "mirror_user"
}

// =============================================================================
//   9. Invocation contract
// =============================================================================
//
// From any DSL context that supports builtins (automations, logic functions,
// policies):
//
//   reply := agent("generalAssistant", {
//     utterance: args.text,
//     spaceContext: { spaceId: args.spaceId, ... },
//     history: args.recentUtterances
//   })
//
//   // reply.response  string  -- the agent's reply text
//   // reply.citations []object -- {domainId, matchedPhrase, ...} entries
//                                  pulled from the agent's respondToUser
//                                  envelope at the end of its tool loop.
//
// The builtin resolves the agent name against the in-memory registry,
// looks up the corresponding v1:agents:agent row (per actor partition + the
// declaration's scope rules), invokes the existing cognition agent dispatch
// pipeline (ForwardTurn -> agent-node tool loop -> respondToUser
// envelope), and returns the envelope as the function value.
//
// The call is synchronous from the DSL caller's perspective. The agent's
// tool loop runs to completion (or errors out) before the call returns.
//
// =============================================================================
//   10. Relationship to v1:agents:agent
// =============================================================================
//
// Every loaded DSL agent declaration MATERIALIZES one or more v1:agents:agent
// rows. The DSL declaration is the SCHEMA + BASELINE; the row is the
// runtime instance. User-personalization fields (avatar, voice id, etc.)
// are added to the row via UI mutations after materialization.
//
// At engine startup the loader:
//
//   1. Walks dsl/agents/v1/**/*.memql and parses each agent declaration.
//   2. For each declaration, registers the agent in the in-memory
//      registry (used by the agent(...) builtin).
//   3. Materializes rows per the @scope annotation:
//      - global  -> one row in _system, ownerUserId="", system-marked.
//      - perUser -> one row per existing v1:identity:user. Then subscribes
//                   to v1:identity:user create events for ongoing
//                   materialization on new-user signup.
//   4. On re-run, stamps DSL-declared field values into existing rows by
//      deterministic id; preserves fields the DSL didn't declare so user
//      personalization survives DSL edits.
//
// Removing an agent file from the tree DOES NOT delete the materialized
// rows. The operator runs an explicit cleanup or marks the agent
// deleted=true via mutation. Implicit row deletion is too dangerous
// (an accidental DSL file deletion would orphan every user's GA).
